\description{
\code{nested_tune_grid()} estimates how well a model tuned with
\code{\link[tune:tune_grid]{tune::tune_grid()}} performs on new data. Each outer fold tunes on its own
inner resamples, and then fits and scores the chosen candidate on the rows
it held out.
}
\details{
For each outer fold, the function calls \code{\link[tune:tune_grid]{tune::tune_grid()}} on the fold's
inner resamples and picks a candidate with \code{select}. It finalizes the
workflow with that candidate. Then \code{\link[tune:last_fit]{tune::last_fit()}} fits the workflow on
the fold's analysis set and scores it on the assessment set.

The estimate from \code{\link[=collect_metrics]{collect_metrics()}} describes this whole procedure (tune,
select, fit) and not one fitted model. To get a model to
deploy, call \code{\link[=nested_final_fit]{nested_final_fit()}}, which runs the same procedure on all
the data. The nested estimate is the number to report for that model.

\code{\link[=nested_tune_bayes]{nested_tune_bayes()}}, \code{\link[=nested_tune_race_anova]{nested_tune_race_anova()}},
\code{\link[=nested_tune_race_win_loss]{nested_tune_race_win_loss()}} and \code{\link[=nested_tune_sim_anneal]{nested_tune_sim_anneal()}} run the same
loop with another search. \code{\link[=nested_fit_resamples]{nested_fit_resamples()}} runs it for a workflow
with nothing to tune. This page documents what they share.

\code{object} can also be a parsnip model specification, given with a
\code{preprocessor}, as in \code{nested_tune_grid(spec, preprocessor, resamples)}.
}
\section{Nested designs}{


\code{resamples} has one row per outer fold: a \code{splits} column of outer splits,
an \code{inner_resamples} column of inner rsets, and id columns. The function
checks the design before it fits anything. An outer bootstrap is an
error, and so are \code{\link[rsample:loo_cv]{rsample::loo_cv()}}, \code{\link[rsample:apparent]{rsample::apparent()}} and
\code{\link[rsample:permutations]{rsample::permutations()}} in either loop. An inner split that reaches a
row its outer fold holds out is an error too. Each error names the rows,
splits or folds at fault.

Time-series designs work as well. \code{nested_tune_grid()} is tested with an
outer \code{\link[rsample:rolling_origin]{rsample::rolling_origin()}}, \code{\link[rsample:sliding_window]{rsample::sliding_window()}},
\code{\link[rsample:sliding_index]{rsample::sliding_index()}} or \code{\link[rsample:sliding_period]{rsample::sliding_period()}} design and an
inner design of any of those four kinds. Keep the \code{lag} of
\code{\link[rsample:rolling_origin]{rsample::rolling_origin()}} at 0. \code{\link[=nested_resamples]{nested_resamples()}} says which of these
designs the other functions are tested on.
}

\section{Finalizing a parameter range}{


A range that depends on the data, such as \code{mtry()}, is finalized on each
outer fold's analysis set, never on the rows that fold holds out.
\code{\link[=nested_final_fit]{nested_final_fit()}} finalizes it on all the data. A range set in
\code{param_info} applies to every fold.
}

\section{Evaluation times}{


\code{eval_time} reaches every tune call that reads it, so a dynamic survival
metric is measured at the times you name. With no such metric in the
set, tune ignores the times with a warning. Each fold selects at the
first of these times.
}

\section{Weighting the outer folds}{


Weights set on the design with \code{\link[tune:add_resample_weights]{tune::add_resample_weights()}} give a
weighted average of the outer folds' estimates, as tune computes it. A
fold that fails or scores \code{NA} is left out, and the other weights are
scaled to sum to one.
}

\section{Reproducibility}{


Call \code{set.seed()} before the function, as elsewhere in tidymodels. Each
fold takes its seeds from that one seed by its position, so the result is
the same serially and in parallel. The seeds are kept as \code{.tuning_seed}
and \code{.outer_fit_seed}. Engines that draw random numbers outside R, such
as kernlab's SVMs, are not covered.
}

\section{When a fold fails}{


A fold that fails does not stop the run. Its \code{.completed} is \code{FALSE} and
its \code{.notes} says which stage failed and why. The run warns at the end,
and \code{\link[=collect_metrics]{collect_metrics()}} summarizes only the folds that completed. If no
fold completed, \code{\link[=collect_metrics]{collect_metrics()}} gives an error.
}

\section{Parallel execution}{


The outer folds run in parallel when two or more mirai daemons are
running, and serially otherwise. Inner tuning always runs serially.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{mirai::daemons(4)
res <- nested_tune_grid(wf, folds, grid = grid)
mirai::daemons(0)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The daemons load nestedtune from an installed library, and they do not
inherit your session's options. Set what a fold needs with
\code{\link[mirai:everywhere]{mirai::everywhere()}}. Before it sends the folds out, the function checks
that every daemon can load the packages the run needs. The check waits
up to 30 seconds, and \verb{options(nestedtune.preflight_timeout = <ms>)}
changes that limit. On a pool with mirai's default dispatcher, an
interrupt cancels the folds that are still running.
}

\section{Differences from calling tune directly}{


A \code{\link[tune:control_grid]{tune::control_grid()}} passed through \code{...} as \code{control} reaches the
inner \code{tune_grid()} in every fold and the outer fit. The result keeps the
control that ran as \code{extract_procedure(res)$control}. Its settings differ
from a direct \code{tune_grid()} call as follows.


\strong{Forced: \code{allow_par}.} The inner tuning run and the outer fit run with
\code{allow_par = FALSE}, because the outer folds carry the parallelism.


\strong{Settable as its own argument: \code{event_level}.} Set the level with the
\code{event_level} argument, which reaches the inner run and the outer fit. A
control that names a level other than tune's default or the argument's is
an error. \code{eval_time} is an argument of \code{tune_grid()} too, not a control
setting.


\strong{Refused: none.} No setting is refused on its own. A control of another
class, such as a \code{control_bayes()}, is an error.


\strong{Passed through: \code{verbose}, \code{pkgs}, \code{parallel_over}, \code{workflow_size}.}
Each reaches \code{tune_grid()} as given. \code{parallel_over} still sets the seed
each model fit starts from, so a stochastic engine's numbers depend on
it.

\strong{Kept from the outer fit: \code{save_pred}, \code{extract}.} With
\code{save_pred = TRUE}, the result has a \code{.predictions} column of each
fold's outer predictions. With an \code{extract} function, it has an
\code{.extracts} column. The inner run's predictions and extracts are still
discarded.
\code{\link[=collect_predictions.nested_results]{collect_predictions()}} and
\code{\link[=collect_predictions.nested_results]{collect_extracts()}} read the two
columns.

\strong{Not returned: \code{save_workflow}.} The inner tuning results are not kept,
so this setting returns nothing. Use \code{extract = function(x) x} to keep
each fold's fitted workflow.


\strong{Inert: \code{backend_options}.} Options for a parallel backend, with no
backend to reach at \code{allow_par = FALSE}.
}

